---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import Code from '../../components/Code.astro';
import MiniFig from '../../components/MiniFig.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site.js';

const parity = `$ npm run test:corpus
55/55  blade cases byte-identical vs PHP 8.3.30`;
---

<Docs
  title="Blade & parity"
  description="Which Blade compiles, how layouts travel, and what byte-parity against the real PHP 8.3 renderer means."
  slug="blade"
  lead="Views are compiled, not interpreted. The reference is the real Laravel renderer running on PHP 8.3, and the comparison is bytes."
  source={`README.md §Compiles today; MAP.md §5 §6b; COMPILED.md — repository ${site.source}`}
>
  <h2 id="what">What compiles</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Blade</th><th scope="col">verdict</th><th scope="col">note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row" class="mono">@extends, @section, @yield</th><td>compiles</td><td>the sections travel to the layout, not just the child view</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row" class="mono">@if, @elseif, @else, @unless</th><td>compiles</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row" class="mono">@foreach, @for, @while, @forelse</th><td>compiles</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row" class="mono">@switch, @match</th><td>compiles</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row" class="mono">ternary, ?:, ??, ??=, isset, empty</th><td>compiles</td><td>evaluated with PHP's own rules for a missing value</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row" class="mono">&#123;&#123; &#125;&#125; and &#123;!! !!&#125;</th><td>compiles</td><td>escaping matches <code>e()</code>, not a regex approximation</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row" class="mono">__('key')</th><td>compiles</td><td>translations are dumped at build time from your <code>lang/</code>. Other translation helpers are not compiled yet and fail loud.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row" class="mono">@vite, @fonts</th><td>compiles</td><td>a runtime shim reads the build manifest — the hashed URLs are the real ones</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row" class="mono">@php blocks</th><td>restricted</td><td>a subset; anything outside it is a build error, never a silent difference</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row" class="mono">&lt;x-…&gt; components, slots</th><td class="bad-text">fails loud</td><td>they compile to a view runtime the edge does not carry → a port, or inline the partial</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row" class="mono">custom directives</th><td class="txt-blocked">not compiled yet</td><td>a directive outside the documented set fails the build; inline the markup or move the work into the view</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <MiniFig name="blade-pipeline" />

  <h2 id="parity">What parity means here</h2>
  <p>
    The build does not “look right” — it compares, in two places. The Blade corpus transpiles each
    case through the CLI, imports the emitted module and byte-compares the rendered output against
    an expectation file that the real Laravel app on PHP 8.3.30 produced. The shadow harness goes
    further and diffs a whole request — status, headers and body — between the PHP app and the
    compiled worker. The corpus is <code>55/55</code> today, and a divergence fails CI rather than
    becoming a footnote.
  </p>
  <Code file="shell" code={parity} />
  <p>
    Two things follow from that. First, the compiled view must reproduce PHP's string and number
    formatting, not JavaScript's — the runtime carries the PHP behaviours (<code>sprintf</code>,
    <code>number_format</code>, <code>json_encode</code>, <code>DateTime</code>) deliberately.
    Second, a Blade construct that cannot be reproduced has exactly one honest answer: the build
    stops.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sharing">Sharing the layout</h2>
  <p>
    A layout is a view too. <code>@extends</code> with <code>@section</code>/<code>@yield</code> is
    compiled as a function that renders the child's sections at the point the layout yields them,
    in the same order Laravel does. Nested includes (<code>@include</code>,
    <code>@includeIf</code>) resolve at build time; a view that does not exist is a build error, not
    an empty string.
  </p>

  <h2 id="locally">Working locally</h2>
  <p>
    Blade stays Blade in development: the same files, the same artisan, the same hot reload. There
    is no second template language and no “edge flavour” of your views. The compiled form only
    exists inside <code>dist/</code>.
  </p>
</Docs>
